# Engineering Task Board

Kanban board: PostgreSQL + a React frontend + one backend (.NET, Python, or Java) behind a shared REST contract.

## Layers

| Layer | Allowed | Never |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Controller / Router | HTTP ↔ domain, map errors to status codes | Business rules, SQL |
| Service | Validation, business rules | HTTP, SQL |
| Repository | All database access | Validation, HTTP |

Frontend: `components/` (presentational) → `pages/` (state + fetching) → `services/` (all HTTP).

```text
BAD:  controller runs SQL or checks a business rule
GOOD: controller calls the service; the service calls the repository
```

## Hard rules

- Do not read `.env`, `.env.local`, or any other `.env.*` file except `.env.example`. Do not read environment variables, API keys, or secrets directly. Use the framework configuration system.
- Schema is owned once, in `database/schema.sql`. Do not add EF migrations, `Base.metadata.create_all()`, or `ddl-auto` values other than `none`.
- `created_at` / `updated_at` are set by the database, never by the client.
- `status` is exactly one of: `todo`, `in-progress`, `done`.
- `404` for a missing id. `422` for a missing title or an unknown status. Do not invent other codes.
- Any new endpoint gets a test in the same layer before the implementation is considered done.

## Conventions

- Match the existing style of the file you are editing. Do not reformat untouched lines.
- Keep the three backends behaviourally identical, with the same request/response shape.
- Prefer editing an existing file over creating a new one.

## Verify

- .NET: `dotnet test` in `backend-dotnet/`
- Python: `pytest` in `backend-python/`
- Java: `./mvnw -B test` in `backend-java/`
- Frontend: `npm test -- --run` in `frontend/`
